// How to Create Server Certificate — MtlsServer.pfx

// Run powershell command to generate a self-signed certificate for the server:
// dotnet dev-certs https -ep MtlsServer.pfx -p MtlsPassword-12345!

// dotnet dev-certs https	Creates a development HTTPS certificate
// -ep MtlsServer.pfx	Export the certificate to a file named MtlsServer.pfx
// -p MtlsPassword-12345!	Protect the file with a password


//The MtlsServer.pfx certificate on your server plays a critical role in the first half of the mTLS handshake and overall security. Its main purposes are:
//1. Server Authentication: 
//      The server presents the MtlsServer.pfx certificate to the client during the TLS handshake. This allows the client to verify the server's identity, ensuring that it is communicating with the intended server and not an imposter.
//2. Encryption: 
//      The certificate contains a public key that is used to establish an encrypted communication channel between the client and server. This ensures that any data exchanged during the session is secure and cannot be intercepted by unauthorized parties.
//3. Integrity: 
//      The certificate also helps ensure the integrity of the data exchanged between the client and server. It allows the client to verify that the data has not been tampered with during transmission.

//Without MtlsServer.pfx, Kestrel wouldn't be able to run over HTTPS, and the server wouldn't have the foundational authority to request a certificate from the client in the first place.